<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pietermaritzburg</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pietermaritzburg is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwazulu-natal">KwaZulu-Natal</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voortrekkers">Voortrekkers</a> in 1838 and named after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boer</a> leaders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piet-retief">Piet Retief</a> and Gerrit Maritz. It was the seat of the Natalia Republic and then of the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colony-of-natal">Colony of Natal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/natal-province">Natal Province</a>. In 1893 Mohandas <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a> was put off a train at its station, an experience that shaped his campaigns against discrimination.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pietermaritzburg.jpg" alt="Pietermaritzburg" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Janek Szymanowski · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:00000-St_Peters_Church2-Pietermaritzburg-s.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Pietermaritzburg is the capital and second-largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwazulu-natal">KwaZulu-Natal</a>, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durban">Durban</a>, about 90 km from the coast on the N3 highway, the main route between Durban and the Pretoria-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/witwatersrand">Witwatersrand</a> conurbation. It is often called Maritzburg or PMB. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> name, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mgungundlovu">uMgungundlovu</a>, comes from King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dingane">Dingane</a>'s royal homestead; the name is popularly translated as Place of the Elephant, though it could also mean The elephant wins. Legend has it that Shaka had his warriors hunt elephants here to sell the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> to English traders at Port Natal, now Durban. The city's coat of arms of 1861 shows an elephant.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voortrekkers">Voortrekkers</a> settled the site in April 1838, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piet-retief">Piet Retief</a> and his party of about seventy were killed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> capital on 6 February 1838. From there the Wenkommando set out in November 1838 and defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dingane">Dingane</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-blood-river">Battle of Blood River</a> on 16 December. Before the battle the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boers</a> vowed to build a church if they won, and the Church of the Vow still stands. There are two accounts of the name. One honours the Voortrekker leaders Piet Retief and Gerrit Maritz; the other holds that it was first named after Retief's full name alone, Pieter Mauritz' Burg. In 1938 the city officially announced that its second element honours Gert Maritz too. Neither man ever reached the town.</p><p>The settlement grew rapidly as the capital of the short-lived <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boer</a> republic of Natalia. Britain took it over in 1843, and it became the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colony-of-natal">Natal Colony</a>'s administration under the first lieutenant-governor, Martin West. Fort Napier was built to house a garrison. When Natal received responsibility for its own government in 1893, an assembly building and a city hall were built. On 1 June 1893 the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a>, travelling to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pretoria">Pretoria</a> with a first-class ticket, refused an order to move to the van compartment and was removed from the train here. He spent the winter night in the station waiting room and decided to stay in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> and fight racial discrimination against Indians, and from that struggle emerged his satyagraha.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>In 1910 Natal became a province of the Union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>. During apartheid the city was segregated, and about 90 per cent of its Indian population was moved to Northdale and most <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> inhabitants to Edendale. After 1994 it shared capital status in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwazulu-natal">KwaZulu-Natal</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulundi">Ulundi</a>, and since 2004 it has been the province's sole capital. A bronze statue of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a> stands in Church Street. The city has a campus of the University of KwaZulu-Natal, whose predecessor, the University of Natal, was founded in 1910 and was one of the first in the country to educate African students. The Comrades Marathon, run since 1921, links Pietermaritzburg and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durban">Durban</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pietermaritzburg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Natal was first run as a district of the Cape Colony and became a separate colony in 1856, governed from <b>Pietermaritzburg</b> with Durban as its port.</blockquote><a href="/state/natal-colony">Natal Colony</a></li><li><blockquote>The trekkers founded <b>Pietermaritzburg</b> and organised a republic with a Volksraad in 1839.</blockquote><a href="/state/natalia-republic">Natalia Republic</a></li></ul></section>
